<!-- SECTION 8: Angle relationship -->
<section id="sec-angle" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Understanding the Relationship Between Secant and Angle Size</h2>
  <p>Secant grows as the reference angle grows. The geometry explains why: when angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> is small, the adjacent side is nearly as long as the hypotenuse, so the ratio sits just above 1. As the angle widens, the adjacent side becomes a smaller share of the hypotenuse, and the ratio climbs. At <span set:html={rich('[[45^\\circ]]')} /> the secant is <span set:html={rich('[[\\sqrt{2}]]')} />, about 1.4142; at <span set:html={rich('[[60^\\circ]]')} /> it is exactly 2, because the hypotenuse is twice the adjacent side; and as the angle approaches <span set:html={rich('[[90^\\circ]]')} />, the adjacent side shrinks toward zero and secant grows without bound.</p>
  <div class="not-prose my-6 overflow-x-auto">
    <table class="w-full min-w-[480px] border-collapse text-left text-sm">
      <thead>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/30">
          <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-on-surface">Angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /></th>
          <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-on-surface">Adjacent side <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /></th>
          <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-on-surface">Hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /></th>
          <th class="py-3 font-semibold text-on-surface">Secant ratio</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="text-on-surface-variant">
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4"><span set:html={rich('[[30^\\circ]]')} /></td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4"><span set:html={rich('[[\\sqrt{3}]]')} /></td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">2</td><td class="py-2.5">Approximately 1.1547</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4"><span set:html={rich('[[45^\\circ]]')} /></td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">1</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4"><span set:html={rich('[[\\sqrt{2}]]')} /></td><td class="py-2.5">Approximately 1.4142</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4"><span set:html={rich('[[60^\\circ]]')} /></td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">1</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">2</td><td class="py-2.5">2</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="py-2.5 pr-4"><span set:html={rich('[[75^\\circ]]')} /></td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">1</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Approximately 3.8637</td><td class="py-2.5">Approximately 3.8637</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>The pattern is monotonic: every increase in the angle raises the secant, and the ratio stays above 1 for every acute angle. The measurements in the table are illustrative; the ratio, not the absolute size of the triangle, determines the angle. Secant therefore describes the triangle's shape, not its scale.</p>

