<!-- SECTION 9: Finding the hypotenuse -->
<section id="sec-hypotenuse" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Find the Hypotenuse Using Secant</h2>
  <p>The secant formula can be rearranged to find the hypotenuse when the adjacent side and the angle are known. Start with <span set:html={rich('[[\\sec(A)=c/b]]')} /> and multiply both sides by <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{c=b\\sec(A)}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The hypotenuse equals the adjacent side multiplied by the secant of the angle. Given an adjacent side of <span set:html={rich('[[b=4\\,\\text{m}]]')} /> and an angle of <span set:html={rich('[[A=60^\\circ]]')} />, and knowing <span set:html={rich('[[\\sec(60^\\circ)=2]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[c=4\\times2]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{c=8\\,\\text{m}}]]')} /></p>
  <p>The hypotenuse is twice the adjacent side at this angle, which matches the 30-60-90 geometry: the side opposite 30 degrees is half the hypotenuse. The adjacent side can be found the same way by rearranging again:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{b=\\frac{c}{\\sec(A)}}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>With a hypotenuse of <span set:html={rich('[[c=10\\,\\text{m}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[A=60^\\circ]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[b=10/2]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{b=5\\,\\text{m}}]]')} />. These are mathematical rearrangements of the same formula. The calculator on this page returns the secant ratio from side lengths; it does not directly calculate missing sides.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 10: Finding an angle -->
<section id="sec-recover" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Find an Angle From a Secant Ratio</h2>
  <p>The secant ratio and the angle are different quantities: one is a dimensionless comparison of sides, the other is measured in degrees. If the secant ratio is known, recover cosine with the reciprocal:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\cos(A)=\\frac{1}{\\sec(A)}]]')} /></p>
  <p>Then inverse cosine returns the angle:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{A=\\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sec(A)}\\right)}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[\\sec(A)=1.25]]')} />, first calculate cosine: <span set:html={rich('[[\\cos(A)=1/1.25=0.8]]')} />. Then <span set:html={rich('[[A=\\cos^{-1}(0.8)]]')} />, giving <span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{A\\approx36.87^\\circ}]]')} />. This is a separate angle calculation, not something the secant ratio calculator performs directly. Set the scientific calculator to degrees if the answer is expected in degrees; in radians mode the same input returns about 0.6435, the same angle in different units.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 11: Common mistakes -->
<section id="sec-mistakes" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Common Mistakes When Calculating Secant</h2>
  <p>Most secant errors come from a flipped fraction, a mislabeled side, or a unit slip. Run through this list before trusting a result.</p>
  <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal space-y-3 pl-6">
    <li><strong>Reversing the formula and calculating <span set:html={rich('[[b/c]]')} /> instead of <span set:html={rich('[[c/b]]')} />.</strong> This is the single most common error, and it returns cosine instead of secant. With <span set:html={rich('[[c=5]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} />, the wrong order gives 0.8; the correct order gives 1.25.</li>
    <li><strong>Confusing secant with cosine.</strong> Cosine is adjacent divided by hypotenuse; secant is the exact reverse. If the answer comes out below 1, it is almost certainly cosine, not secant.</li>
    <li><strong>Confusing secant with cosecant.</strong> Both use the hypotenuse in the numerator, so they look similar. Secant divides by the adjacent side <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />; cosecant divides by the opposite side <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />. Check which leg the problem actually gives.</li>
    <li><strong>Using the opposite side instead of the adjacent side.</strong> Dividing <span set:html={rich('[[c/a]]')} /> computes cosecant. With <span set:html={rich('[[c=5]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[a=3]]')} />, that gives 1.6667, a correct cosecant but a wrong secant.</li>
    <li><strong>Using a side that is not the hypotenuse in the numerator.</strong> Secant always has the hypotenuse on top. A leg over a leg is tangent or cotangent; a leg over the hypotenuse is sine or cosine.</li>
    <li><strong>Mixing up adjacent and opposite sides.</strong> The labels swap when the reference angle changes from <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> to <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} />. Always confirm which angle the problem names before assigning <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />.</li>
    <li><strong>Assuming secant is an angle measured in degrees.</strong> A result of 1.25 is a ratio, not <span set:html={rich('[[1.25^\\circ]]')} />. Treating it as degrees feeds nonsense into any follow-up calculation.</li>
    <li><strong>Reporting the ratio with metres or centimetres.</strong> The units cancel in the division, so "1.25 m" is wrong. Write the plain number and keep the units in the input measurements where they belong.</li>
    <li><strong>Entering zero or negative side lengths.</strong> A triangle cannot have a zero or negative side, so these inputs signal a data entry error or a missing measurement, not a valid edge case.</li>
    <li><strong>Entering an adjacent side longer than the hypotenuse.</strong> The hypotenuse is always the longest side. If <span set:html={rich('[[b \\gt c]]')} />, the inputs are swapped or the triangle is impossible.</li>
    <li><strong>Mixing measurement units.</strong> A hypotenuse of 5 m with an adjacent side of 40 cm gives 0.125, a meaningless number. Convert to a single unit first: 5 m and 4 m give the correct 1.25.</li>
    <li><strong>Rounding too early.</strong> Rounding an intermediate division to two decimals and then continuing the calculation compounds the error. Keep full precision through every step and round only the final answer.</li>
    <li><strong>Assuming secant must equal 1 for a right triangle.</strong> No right triangle has a secant of exactly 1. Expect a value above 1, and treat exactly 1 as a signal that the inputs were reversed or the triangle is degenerate.</li>
    <li><strong>Confusing reciprocal cosine with inverse cosine.</strong> <span set:html={rich('[[1/\\cos(A)]]')} /> is secant, a ratio; <span set:html={rich('[[\\cos^{-1}]]')} /> is inverse cosine, which returns an angle. The similar names cause real errors, so read the notation carefully.</li>
    <li><strong>Assuming secant alone determines the triangle's absolute dimensions.</strong> Secant describes shape, not size: infinitely many triangles share a secant of 1.25, from a 5 cm hypotenuse to a 5 km one. The ratio fixes the proportions; actual lengths need at least one side measurement.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Wrong Versus Correct: A Worked Comparison</h3>
  <p>Given a hypotenuse of <span set:html={rich('[[c=5]]')} /> and an adjacent side of <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} />:</p>
  <p><strong>Incorrect:</strong> <span set:html={rich('[[4/5=0.8]]')} />. This is cosine, not secant. The numerator and denominator are reversed.</p>
  <p><strong>Correct:</strong> <span set:html={rich('[[\\sec(A)=5/4=1.25]]')} /></p>
  <p><strong>Verify:</strong> <span set:html={rich('[[\\cos(A)=0.8]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[1/0.8=1.25]]')} />. The reciprocal check confirms the result, and the answer sits above 1, as every valid secant must.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 12: Verification -->
<section id="sec-verify" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Check Whether Your Secant Result Is Valid</h2>
  <p>A valid, non-degenerate right triangle requires positive sides with the hypotenuse strictly longest, <span set:html={rich('[[0 \\lt b \\lt c]]')} />, so the secant result must be greater than 1:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{\\sec(A)=\\frac{c}{b} \\gt 1}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Both measurements must use compatible units. A result at or below 1 usually indicates reversed inputs, incorrect side identification, incompatible measurements, or invalid triangle dimensions.</p>
  <h3>Method 1: Check the Reciprocal of Cosine</h3>
  <p>Verify <span set:html={rich('[[\\cos(A)=b/c]]')} />, then confirm <span set:html={rich('[[\\sec(A)=1/\\cos(A)]]')} />. With <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c=5]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[\\cos(A)=4/5=0.8]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[\\sec(A)=1/0.8=1.25]]')} />. A mismatch between the two paths signals a reversed fraction or a mislabeled side.</p>
  <h3>Method 2: Check the Pythagorean Theorem</h3>
  <p>If the opposite side is known or needed, calculate <span set:html={rich('[[a=\\sqrt{c^2-b^2}]]')} />. For <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c=5]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[a=\\sqrt{25-16}=3]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[3^2+4^2=5^2]]')} />. This confirms the dimensions of the 3-4-5 right triangle. The opposite side is a verification value here, not part of the primary secant formula.</p>
  <h3>Method 3: Check Using Inverse Cosine</h3>
  <p>For a secant ratio of 1.25: <span set:html={rich('[[A=\\cos^{-1}(1/1.25)]]')} />, which is <span set:html={rich('[[A=\\cos^{-1}(0.8)\\approx36.87^\\circ]]')} />. Because the secant sits modestly above 1, the angle should land well below <span set:html={rich('[[45^\\circ]]')} />; if it does not, the side labels are probably swapped. Small differences may arise from rounding.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 13: Choosing a ratio -->
<section id="sec-vs" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Secant vs. Cosine, Sine, and Cosecant</h2>
  <p>Each ratio compares a different pair of sides, using the same reference angle and side labels:</p>
  <div class="not-prose my-6 overflow-x-auto">
    <table class="w-full min-w-[480px] border-collapse text-left text-sm">
      <thead>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/30">
          <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-on-surface">Ratio</th>
          <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-on-surface">Formula</th>
          <th class="py-3 font-semibold text-on-surface">What it compares</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="text-on-surface-variant">
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Sine</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">sin(A) = a/c</td><td class="py-2.5">Opposite side to hypotenuse</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Cosine</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">cos(A) = b/c</td><td class="py-2.5">Adjacent side to hypotenuse</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Tangent</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">tan(A) = a/b</td><td class="py-2.5">Opposite side to adjacent side</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Secant</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">sec(A) = c/b</td><td class="py-2.5">Hypotenuse to adjacent side</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Cosecant</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">csc(A) = c/a</td><td class="py-2.5">Hypotenuse to opposite side</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Cotangent</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">cot(A) = b/a</td><td class="py-2.5">Adjacent side to opposite side</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>Secant and cosine are the reciprocal pair: <span set:html={rich('[[\\sec(A)=1/\\cos(A)]]')} />. If <span set:html={rich('[[\\cos(A)=0.8]]')} />, then <span set:html={rich('[[\\sec(A)=1.25]]')} />. Reach for secant when a formula calls for hypotenuse divided by adjacent, when you are comparing a sloping length with its horizontal projection, or when the known relationship starts from the longest side; reach for cosine for adjacent over hypotenuse, sine for the opposite side against the hypotenuse, and cosecant when the hypotenuse is compared with the opposite side.</p>
  <p>For the neighbouring ratios, see the <a href="/sine-ratio-calculator/" class="text-primary hover:underline">Sine Ratio Calculator</a>, the <a href="/cosine-ratio-calculator/" class="text-primary hover:underline">Cosine Ratio Calculator</a>, the <a href="/tangent-ratio-calculator/" class="text-primary hover:underline">Tangent Ratio Calculator</a>, the <a href="/cosecant-ratio-calculator/" class="text-primary hover:underline">Cosecant Ratio Calculator</a>, and the <a href="/cotangent-ratio-calculator/" class="text-primary hover:underline">Cotangent Ratio Calculator</a>.</p>
</section>

